courses.learning_outcomes:
Upon completing this course, students should be able to:
1. Give an overview of the origins of certain selected biological and physiological signals;
2. Understand the design process of various medical devices;
3. Evaluate the different classes of medical instruments and measurement principles;
4. Master some common medical instruments;
5. Foresee the future trend in related topics;
6. Design at least one new medical device that meets health demand;
7. Demonstrate ability to design new medical devices in a dynamic group setting; and
8. Have acquired design-thinking skills in producing prototypes that meet specific design goals.

Upon completion of this course, students are expected to able to:
1. understand the tools and concepts of biomolecular engineering;
2. interpret data from and formulate hypothesis based on research in biomolecular engineering and offer objective critiques to the scientific literature;
3. appreciate the impact of biomolecular engineering from intellectual, clinical and commercial perspectives

By the end of the course, students should be able to:
1. Clearly define and differentiate between gene therapy and genetic engineering;
2. Explain the principles of antisense technology and its therapeutic potential;
3. Demonstrate an understanding of gene editing technologies and their clinical implications; and
4. Evaluate case studies of gene therapy applications, particularly in the context of biomedical engineering.

After completing this course, students will be able to:
1. Understand the basic concepts of stem cells: master the definition, criteria and standards of "stem cells" and understand their importance in biomedicine;
2. Apply the principles of stem cell biology: be able to explain the basic biological mechanisms of stem cells, including the characteristics of embryonic stem cells, fetal stem cells and adult stem cells and their development process;
3. Analyze the application of stem cell technology: identify and discuss the main application areas of stem cells in regenerative medicine, such as cardiac, neurological, orthopedic and immunotherapy;
4. Evaluate ethical and regulatory issues: understand the ethical considerations and laws and regulations related to stem cell research, and be able to put forward reasonable opinions in relevant discussions;
5. Ability to solve practical problems: transform biological science knowledge into innovative solutions, and be able to propose practical stem cell technology applications for real-world challenges;
6. Conduct scientific discussions and exchanges: have the ability to participate in meaningful discussions about the application and future development potential of stem cells, and be able to conduct effective scientific exchanges with peers; and
7. Prepare for a career: through a comprehensive understanding of the field of stem cells, enhance the ability to develop careers in biomedical engineering and related fields, and lay the foundation for future research or careers.
